1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,692
Why would thousands of people stand in

2
00:00:02,692 --> 00:00:04,227
the snow before dawn,

3
00:00:04,227 --> 00:00:07,324
in the capital of the Soviet Union,

4
00:00:07,324 --> 00:00:09,745
just to buy a hamburger?

5
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:11,902
On January thirty-first,

6
00:00:11,902 --> 00:00:13,577
nineteen ninety,

7
00:00:13,577 --> 00:00:16,249
people began lining up near Pushkin Square

8
00:00:16,249 --> 00:00:18,434
in Moscow as early as four in

9
00:00:18,434 --> 00:00:19,763
the morning.

10
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:21,503
By the end of the day,

11
00:00:21,503 --> 00:00:23,720
thirty thousand customers had walked

12
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,493
through the doors of the first McDonald's

13
00:00:25,493 --> 00:00:27,023
in the Soviet Union,

14
00:00:27,023 --> 00:00:29,461
a record for any opening day in

15
00:00:29,461 --> 00:00:31,613
the company's history.

16
00:00:31,853 --> 00:00:32,979
For a few years,

17
00:00:32,979 --> 00:00:35,505
that hamburger became a symbol of the

18
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:37,713
Cold War coming to an end.

19
00:00:37,913 --> 00:00:39,457
Thirty-two years later,

20
00:00:39,457 --> 00:00:42,519
it became a symbol of something else.

21
00:00:53,183 --> 00:00:56,102
How do you open a burger restaurant

22
00:00:56,102 --> 00:00:59,021
in a country where private business is

23
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:00,683
not allowed?

24
00:01:02,183 --> 00:01:03,893
Very slowly.

25
00:01:04,113 --> 00:01:06,130
The idea started in the nineteen

26
00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:06,917
seventies,

27
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:09,333
with the Canadian arm of McDonald's.

28
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:11,997
Beginning in nineteen seventy-six,

29
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:14,183
one of its executives made the first

30
00:01:14,183 --> 00:01:15,951
of dozens of trips to Moscow,

31
00:01:15,951 --> 00:01:18,336
to lobby the officials of the Communist

32
00:01:18,336 --> 00:01:19,333
Party.

33
00:01:19,613 --> 00:01:21,298
In the Soviet Union,

34
00:01:21,298 --> 00:01:22,983
the state owned the shops,

35
00:01:22,983 --> 00:01:25,293
the farms and the factories.

36
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:27,900
A foreign company could not just rent

37
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,157
a building and start selling.

38
00:01:30,417 --> 00:01:33,040
The door opened only when the rules

39
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,017
changed.

40
00:01:34,017 --> 00:01:35,849
In the late nineteen eighties,

41
00:01:35,849 --> 00:01:37,270
under perestroika,

42
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:39,534
the Soviet Union began to allow joint

43
00:01:39,534 --> 00:01:41,987
ventures with foreign companies.

44
00:01:42,187 --> 00:01:43,656
On April twenty-ninth,

45
00:01:43,656 --> 00:01:45,338
nineteen eighty-seven,

46
00:01:45,338 --> 00:01:47,467
McDonald's Canada signed one with the

47
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:49,557
Moscow city government.

48
00:01:49,657 --> 00:01:51,257
But there was a catch.

49
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:53,803
The city kept the controlling share,

50
00:01:53,803 --> 00:01:55,427
fifty-one percent.

51
00:01:55,477 --> 00:01:57,383
The foreign partner was limited to

52
00:01:57,383 --> 00:01:58,554
forty-nine.

53
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:01,647
Let me draw how that deal worked.

54
00:02:01,647 --> 00:02:02,173
On one side,

55
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:03,103
the city of Moscow,

56
00:02:03,103 --> 00:02:04,067
with fifty-one percent.

57
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:05,335
On the other,

58
00:02:05,335 --> 00:02:06,862
McDonald's Canada,

59
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:08,187
with forty-nine.

60
00:02:08,187 --> 00:02:08,952
Between them,

61
00:02:08,952 --> 00:02:09,914
one company,

62
00:02:09,914 --> 00:02:11,607
which would own the restaurant.

63
00:02:11,607 --> 00:02:13,631
The city brought the land,

64
00:02:13,631 --> 00:02:17,457
the permits and the political cover.

65
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:18,279
McDonald's brought the money,

66
00:02:18,279 --> 00:02:19,557
the recipes and the know-how.

67
00:02:19,557 --> 00:02:22,067
And neither could do it alone.

68
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:23,325
In the end,

69
00:02:23,325 --> 00:02:25,366
it took fourteen years of work,

70
00:02:25,366 --> 00:02:27,950
and an investment of fifty million

71
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:28,700
dollars,

72
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:31,367
before the first burger was sold.

73
00:02:31,677 --> 00:02:33,401
And a big part of that money

74
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:35,557
did not go into the restaurant at

75
00:02:35,557 --> 00:02:36,372
all.

76
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:39,202
Because there was a problem nobody could

77
00:02:39,202 --> 00:02:40,977
negotiate away.

78
00:02:41,337 --> 00:02:44,196
The country had no reliable supply of

79
00:02:44,196 --> 00:02:46,615
the food a burger chain needs.

80
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:54,243
What do you do when the country

81
00:02:54,243 --> 00:02:56,835
cannot supply your ingredients?

82
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:03,796
A hamburger chain depends on the same

83
00:03:03,796 --> 00:03:04,279
bun,

84
00:03:04,279 --> 00:03:06,800
the same beef and the same potato,

85
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,047
every day,

86
00:03:08,047 --> 00:03:09,920
in huge quantities.

87
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:12,047
In the Soviet planned economy,

88
00:03:12,047 --> 00:03:14,040
nobody could guarantee that.

89
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:16,908
So McDonald's decided to build the supply

90
00:03:16,908 --> 00:03:18,262
chain itself.

91
00:03:18,517 --> 00:03:20,695
In nineteen eighty-nine,

92
00:03:20,695 --> 00:03:22,901
work began on a giant food plant

93
00:03:22,901 --> 00:03:24,253
in Solntsevo,

94
00:03:24,253 --> 00:03:26,507
on the edge of Moscow.

95
00:03:26,627 --> 00:03:28,962
It covered about one hundred thousand

96
00:03:28,962 --> 00:03:29,987
square feet.

97
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:31,681
According to CBC,

98
00:03:31,681 --> 00:03:33,762
it was built to turn out fourteen

99
00:03:33,762 --> 00:03:35,296
thousand buns an hour,

100
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:37,977
and ten thousand patties an hour.

101
00:03:38,067 --> 00:03:40,860
The building opened in nineteen ninety,

102
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:43,068
just in time for the restaurant.

103
00:03:43,317 --> 00:03:46,442
Even the potatoes had to be imported,

104
00:03:46,442 --> 00:03:47,527
as seeds.

105
00:03:47,577 --> 00:03:49,849
McDonald's brought in Russet Burbank seed

106
00:03:49,849 --> 00:03:50,834
potatoes,

107
00:03:50,834 --> 00:03:53,333
the variety used for its fries,

108
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:56,110
and trained Soviet collective farmers in

109
00:03:56,110 --> 00:03:58,477
how to grow bigger harvests.

110
00:03:58,657 --> 00:04:00,970
A burger restaurant was teaching farming

111
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:02,831
to a superpower.

112
00:04:03,083 --> 00:04:05,183
Let me draw the chain.

113
00:04:05,213 --> 00:04:06,327
Here are the farms,

114
00:04:06,327 --> 00:04:08,973
with the imported potato seeds.

115
00:04:09,053 --> 00:04:11,120
The crop goes into the plant,

116
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:12,663
which makes the buns,

117
00:04:12,663 --> 00:04:14,833
the patties and the fries.

118
00:04:14,883 --> 00:04:16,040
And from the plant,

119
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:18,165
the food goes to the restaurant on

120
00:04:18,165 --> 00:04:19,473
Pushkin Square.

121
00:04:19,613 --> 00:04:21,661
Every link in the chain was built

122
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:22,321
from scratch,

123
00:04:22,321 --> 00:04:25,425
before a single customer walked in.

124
00:04:25,683 --> 00:04:28,853
By the end of January nineteen ninety,

125
00:04:28,853 --> 00:04:30,593
the chain was ready.

126
00:04:30,773 --> 00:04:33,454
Now the question was whether anyone would

127
00:04:33,454 --> 00:04:34,322
come.

128
00:04:39,983 --> 00:04:42,481
How many people will queue in the

129
00:04:42,481 --> 00:04:45,024
snow for a taste of the West?

130
00:04:48,983 --> 00:04:50,382
The answer was,

131
00:04:50,382 --> 00:04:51,553
thousands.

132
00:04:51,583 --> 00:04:53,308
On January thirty-first,

133
00:04:53,308 --> 00:04:55,711
the first people arrived around four in

134
00:04:55,711 --> 00:04:56,563
the morning,

135
00:04:56,563 --> 00:04:58,473
in the dark and the cold.

136
00:04:58,643 --> 00:05:00,461
By the time the doors opened,

137
00:05:00,461 --> 00:05:03,103
thousands of people were waiting in line.

138
00:05:03,363 --> 00:05:04,019
Inside,

139
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:06,281
the restaurant had twenty-seven cash

140
00:05:06,281 --> 00:05:08,465
registers ready for them.

141
00:05:08,717 --> 00:05:11,492
The staff had expected to serve around

142
00:05:11,492 --> 00:05:13,757
ten thousand meals that day.

143
00:05:13,897 --> 00:05:16,217
They served thirty thousand.

144
00:05:16,497 --> 00:05:19,098
No McDonald's anywhere in the world had

145
00:05:19,098 --> 00:05:22,220
ever served so many customers on its

146
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:23,340
first day.

147
00:05:23,583 --> 00:05:26,538
Let me draw what opening day looked

148
00:05:26,538 --> 00:05:27,433
like.

149
00:05:27,503 --> 00:05:28,852
Here is the plan,

150
00:05:28,852 --> 00:05:30,553
ten thousand meals.

151
00:05:30,643 --> 00:05:32,883
And here is what actually happened.

152
00:05:33,103 --> 00:05:34,813
Thirty thousand.

153
00:05:34,813 --> 00:05:36,353
Three times the plan,

154
00:05:36,353 --> 00:05:38,416
from a single restaurant,

155
00:05:38,416 --> 00:05:40,321
in a single day.

156
00:05:40,583 --> 00:05:42,483
And it was not cheap.

157
00:05:42,483 --> 00:05:44,016
According to CBC,

158
00:05:44,016 --> 00:05:46,094
a meal cost about half a day's

159
00:05:46,094 --> 00:05:47,993
average wages.

160
00:05:48,233 --> 00:05:50,552
One man outside told a reporter that

161
00:05:50,552 --> 00:05:52,543
they were being ripped off.

162
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:53,320
Still,

163
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,463
the crowds kept coming.

164
00:05:55,573 --> 00:05:56,787
For many people,

165
00:05:56,787 --> 00:05:59,239
it was not really about the food.

166
00:05:59,483 --> 00:06:01,801
The restaurant's first manager put it

167
00:06:01,801 --> 00:06:02,633
best.

168
00:06:02,833 --> 00:06:05,588
Many people talk about perestroika,

169
00:06:05,588 --> 00:06:06,259
he said,

170
00:06:06,259 --> 00:06:08,943
but for them it is an abstraction.

171
00:06:09,283 --> 00:06:11,663
I can touch my perestroika.

172
00:06:11,713 --> 00:06:13,253
I can taste it.

173
00:06:13,513 --> 00:06:16,341
Big Mac is perestroika.

174
00:06:16,583 --> 00:06:19,324
Even the country's leadership saw it that

175
00:06:19,324 --> 00:06:20,173
way.

176
00:06:20,283 --> 00:06:22,409
Mikhail Gorbachev later called the

177
00:06:22,409 --> 00:06:23,876
restaurant a breakthrough,

178
00:06:23,876 --> 00:06:26,001
one which showed that new economic

179
00:06:26,001 --> 00:06:28,552
relations between the Soviet Union and the

180
00:06:28,552 --> 00:06:30,853
rest of the world were possible.

181
00:06:30,883 --> 00:06:33,004
A hamburger had become a piece of

182
00:06:33,004 --> 00:06:34,665
foreign policy.

183
00:06:34,917 --> 00:06:37,130
Less than two years later,

184
00:06:37,130 --> 00:06:40,617
the Soviet Union itself was gone.

185
00:06:40,727 --> 00:06:42,961
The restaurant stayed.

186
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:51,100
What happens to a symbol when the

187
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:53,631
country around it changes?

188
00:06:57,617 --> 00:06:59,488
In the new Russia,

189
00:06:59,488 --> 00:07:02,307
McDonald's slowly took control.

190
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:05,289
In February nineteen ninety-six,

191
00:07:05,289 --> 00:07:07,423
it bought most of the city's shares

192
00:07:07,423 --> 00:07:08,823
in the joint venture,

193
00:07:08,823 --> 00:07:10,957
raising its own stake from forty-nine

194
00:07:10,957 --> 00:07:13,217
percent to eighty percent.

195
00:07:13,347 --> 00:07:15,318
The company that had once needed

196
00:07:15,318 --> 00:07:18,164
permission to open a single restaurant now

197
00:07:18,164 --> 00:07:19,859
ran the business.

198
00:07:20,117 --> 00:07:21,337
And it grew.

199
00:07:21,337 --> 00:07:22,858
By twenty twenty-two,

200
00:07:22,858 --> 00:07:24,843
McDonald's had more than eight hundred and

201
00:07:24,843 --> 00:07:27,207
fifty restaurants in Russia.

202
00:07:27,237 --> 00:07:29,525
It owned about eighty-four percent of

203
00:07:29,525 --> 00:07:29,920
them,

204
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,563
and ran most of them itself,

205
00:07:31,563 --> 00:07:33,827
rather than through franchise owners.

206
00:07:33,857 --> 00:07:36,787
They employed sixty-two thousand people.

207
00:07:36,877 --> 00:07:39,359
Together with its restaurants in Ukraine,

208
00:07:39,359 --> 00:07:41,653
the business brought in about nine percent

209
00:07:41,653 --> 00:07:43,717
of the company's annual revenue,

210
00:07:43,717 --> 00:07:45,777
around two billion dollars.

211
00:07:45,997 --> 00:07:48,009
For a brand built on burgers and

212
00:07:48,009 --> 00:07:48,486
fries,

213
00:07:48,486 --> 00:07:50,749
Russia had become one of its big

214
00:07:50,749 --> 00:07:51,852
markets.

215
00:07:52,117 --> 00:07:55,217
Let me draw how the business changed.

216
00:07:55,237 --> 00:07:56,924
In nineteen ninety,

217
00:07:56,924 --> 00:07:57,971
one restaurant,

218
00:07:57,971 --> 00:07:59,658
forty-nine percent owned,

219
00:07:59,658 --> 00:08:01,967
in a joint venture with the state.

220
00:08:02,077 --> 00:08:03,728
By twenty twenty-two,

221
00:08:03,728 --> 00:08:05,642
more than eight hundred and fifty

222
00:08:05,642 --> 00:08:06,575
restaurants,

223
00:08:06,575 --> 00:08:09,807
mostly owned and run by McDonald's itself.

224
00:08:10,047 --> 00:08:12,000
That is what made the next step

225
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,715
so expensive.

226
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:14,457
Then,

227
00:08:14,457 --> 00:08:17,100
in February twenty twenty-two,

228
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:19,569
Russia invaded Ukraine.

229
00:08:25,217 --> 00:08:27,664
What does it cost to walk away

230
00:08:27,664 --> 00:08:30,711
from thirty years of business?

231
00:08:34,217 --> 00:08:35,071
On March eighth,

232
00:08:35,071 --> 00:08:36,362
twenty twenty-two,

233
00:08:36,362 --> 00:08:38,113
McDonald's announced that it was

234
00:08:38,113 --> 00:08:40,739
temporarily closing its restaurants in

235
00:08:40,739 --> 00:08:41,777
Russia.

236
00:08:42,097 --> 00:08:43,318
Two months later,

237
00:08:43,318 --> 00:08:44,540
on May sixteenth,

238
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:46,137
it went further.

239
00:08:46,197 --> 00:08:48,253
After more than thirty years in the

240
00:08:48,253 --> 00:08:48,916
country,

241
00:08:48,916 --> 00:08:49,578
it said,

242
00:08:49,578 --> 00:08:51,383
it would leave Russia for good,

243
00:08:51,383 --> 00:08:53,439
and it expected to book a charge

244
00:08:53,439 --> 00:08:55,266
of about one point two to one

245
00:08:55,266 --> 00:08:57,237
point four billion dollars.

246
00:08:57,437 --> 00:09:00,151
Its chief executive wrote that the company

247
00:09:00,151 --> 00:09:02,412
could no longer keep the Arches shining

248
00:09:02,412 --> 00:09:03,239
there.

249
00:09:03,483 --> 00:09:04,635
Three days later,

250
00:09:04,635 --> 00:09:06,751
McDonald's agreed to sell the whole

251
00:09:06,751 --> 00:09:09,314
business to Alexander Govor,

252
00:09:09,314 --> 00:09:11,666
a local businessman who had run

253
00:09:11,666 --> 00:09:14,487
twenty-five restaurants in Siberia as a

254
00:09:14,487 --> 00:09:16,263
McDonald's licensee.

255
00:09:16,803 --> 00:09:19,152
The deal kept the employees in their

256
00:09:19,152 --> 00:09:21,513
jobs for at least two years.

257
00:09:21,563 --> 00:09:24,322
The price was never made public.

258
00:09:24,583 --> 00:09:27,583
Let me draw what leaving cost.

259
00:09:27,793 --> 00:09:28,971
Here is McDonald's,

260
00:09:28,971 --> 00:09:31,350
and here is the Russian business,

261
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:33,272
more than eight hundred and fifty

262
00:09:33,272 --> 00:09:34,593
restaurants.

263
00:09:34,773 --> 00:09:36,767
An arrow to the new owner,

264
00:09:36,767 --> 00:09:39,383
for a price nobody disclosed.

265
00:09:39,453 --> 00:09:40,707
And an arrow back,

266
00:09:40,707 --> 00:09:42,543
to the company's books.

267
00:09:42,543 --> 00:09:44,475
A charge of one point three billion

268
00:09:44,475 --> 00:09:45,176
dollars,

269
00:09:45,176 --> 00:09:47,109
mostly to write off what it had

270
00:09:47,109 --> 00:09:49,158
invested in Russia.

271
00:09:49,417 --> 00:09:50,557
In its results,

272
00:09:50,557 --> 00:09:52,651
McDonald's booked a charge of one point

273
00:09:52,651 --> 00:09:55,210
two billion dollars in the second quarter

274
00:09:55,210 --> 00:09:56,816
of twenty twenty-two,

275
00:09:56,816 --> 00:09:59,142
and one point three billion dollars over

276
00:09:59,142 --> 00:10:00,748
the first half of the year,

277
00:10:00,748 --> 00:10:03,540
related to the sale of its business

278
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:04,838
in Russia.

279
00:10:05,083 --> 00:10:08,373
And then came the strangest part.

280
00:10:08,393 --> 00:10:09,376
On June twelfth,

281
00:10:09,376 --> 00:10:10,862
twenty twenty-two,

282
00:10:10,862 --> 00:10:13,633
the restaurants began to reopen under

283
00:10:13,633 --> 00:10:14,868
their new owner,

284
00:10:14,868 --> 00:10:17,387
starting with the very same building on

285
00:10:17,387 --> 00:10:18,723
Pushkin Square.

286
00:10:18,723 --> 00:10:19,914
Same address,

287
00:10:19,914 --> 00:10:21,717
and under the deal,

288
00:10:21,717 --> 00:10:23,233
the same staff.

289
00:10:23,463 --> 00:10:25,843
Only the name had changed.

290
00:10:25,963 --> 00:10:27,763
It was announced just a couple of

291
00:10:27,763 --> 00:10:29,803
hours before the doors opened.

292
00:10:29,973 --> 00:10:32,353
Vkusno and tochka.

293
00:10:32,353 --> 00:10:33,265
Roughly,

294
00:10:33,265 --> 00:10:34,178
tasty,

295
00:10:34,178 --> 00:10:35,721
and that's it.

296
00:10:35,983 --> 00:10:38,103
So what does one hamburger restaurant

297
00:10:38,103 --> 00:10:40,823
teach us about the world economy?

298
00:10:41,083 --> 00:10:43,283
That a business is never only a

299
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:44,483
business.

300
00:10:44,673 --> 00:10:46,582
In nineteen ninety,

301
